how to push this onto github repo
To push your code directory (ltiws) onto a GitHub repository, follow these step-by-step instructions.
Step 1: Initialize Git and Stage Your Files
Run these commands inside your project root directory (~/ltiws) to turn it into a Git repository and stage your files:
bash

# Make sure you are in the root project folder (not inside src)
cd ~/ltiws

# Initialize an empty Git repository
git init

# (Optional but Recommended) Create a .gitignore file to exclude temporary database files
echo "*.db" >> .gitignore

# Stage all files for the first commit
git add .

# Commit the files with a descriptive message
git commit -m "Initial commit: OLTP/OLAP scripts and sample files"

Use code with caution.
Step 2: Create a New Repository on GitHub

    Open your web browser and go to GitHub.
    Log in and click the + icon in the top right corner, then select New repository.
    Name your repository (e.g., ltiws).
    Do not check any boxes for Add a README file, Add .gitignore, or Choose a license (keeping it blank prevents merge conflicts).
    Click Create repository.

Step 3: Link and Push to GitHub
GitHub will show you a page with setup commands. Copy and run the following lines in your terminal to link your local folder to the remote GitHub cloud:
bash

# Rename your default local branch to 'main'
git branch -M main

# Link your local repository to your remote GitHub repository
# (Replace 'YOUR_USERNAME' and 'YOUR_REPO_NAME' with your actual GitHub details)
git remote add origin https://github.com

# Push your code to GitHub
git push -u origin main

Use code with caution.
Alternative: Authenticating with a Personal Access Token (PAT)
If GitHub rejects your password during the git push step, it is because GitHub requires a Personal Access Token instead of your regular password for terminal authentication:

    On GitHub, go to Settings → Developer Settings → Personal Access Tokens → Tokens (classic).
    Click Generate new token (classic), check the repo scope box, and generate it.
    Copy the token. When your terminal prompts you for a password during git push, paste this token instead of your regular password.

